ronment that has favored Democrats, which keeps this at the Likely rather than Safe end.", "watch": "A strong national Democratic wave or a serious misstep by Wied on issues like tariffs could pull the margin closer than current ratings suggest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tony Wied (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's 8th District, anchored in Green Bay and the Fox Valley, leans Republican by about eight points. First-term Representative Tony Wied enters the general election with the advantages of incumbency, roughly $1.1 million raised, and more than half a million dollars on hand. Democrat Rick Crosson won a three-way primary in August but has raised about $75,000, leaving him heavily outspent.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on district fundamentals, money, and outside assessments. Every major rating service places the seat in Safe, Solid, or Likely Republican territory, and prediction markets give Wied roughly a 90 percent chance of winning. A national environment favorable to Democrats is the main source of uncertainty, but the district's lean, the money gap, and Wied's incumbency make him a clear favorite heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0582, "margin": -10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.052, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0699, "margin": -12.0}]}